<Project Sdk="Microsoft.NET.Sdk">

  <PropertyGroup>
    <RootNamespace>FlowX.RabbitMq.Tests</RootNamespace>
  </PropertyGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.NET.Test.Sdk" Version="18.11.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.v3" Version="4.0.2" />
    <PackageReference Include="xunit.runner.visualstudio" Version="4.0.1" />
    <PackageReference Include="Shouldly" Version="4.3.0" />
    <PackageReference Include="coverlet.collector" Version="10.1.0" />
    <!--
      The container implementation, not just its abstractions: the registration test builds a
      provider and asks which service types AddFlowXRabbitMq actually produced. Asserting on the
      ServiceDescriptor list instead would assert on what was written down rather than on what a
      host resolves. The same reasoning, and the same pin, as FlowX.Redis.Tests.
    -->
    <PackageReference Include="Microsoft.Extensions.DependencyInjection" Version="10.0.0" />
  </ItemGroup>

  <ItemGroup>
    <ProjectReference Include="../../plugins/FlowX.RabbitMq/FlowX.RabbitMq.csproj" />

    <!--
      The host, because the bus trigger is the one thing in this package that only means something
      in a host: a consumer that reads a message and does not start a flow has proved nothing about
      redelivery, acknowledgement or ordering. RabbitMqBusConsumerTests drives FlowBusScan over a
      real broker, which is the only place the two halves meet. The dependency is one-way and stays
      inside tests/ — no plugin references a host.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../../src/FlowX.Hosting/FlowX.Hosting.csproj" />

    <!--
      The conformance suite is referenced, not copied, and this project is the reason the reference
      is worth having a third time. FlowX.Redis.Tests was the second derivation and showed the
      suite is store-independent; this is the second *transport*, and PublisherConformance passes
      unchanged against a broker whose ordering mechanism has nothing in common with a Redis
      stream. Nothing in tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests changed to make it work, which is the
      finding this package was commissioned to produce.
    -->
    <ProjectReference Include="../FlowX.Conformance.Tests/FlowX.Conformance.Tests.csproj" />
  </ItemGroup>

</Project>
